WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.834
-I'm so happy to see you,
as always.

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.334
Little Marlowe, your daughter,

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:05.667
is so beautiful and cute
backstage.

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:07.067
-Staying up late
on a school night.

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:08.734
-I know, my gosh.
-Couldn't keep her away.

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:10.934
-Oh, I'm so happy, yeah.
And I'm so happy we had puppies

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:13.033
here so that she could play with
the puppies, too.

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:16.067
It's Nana Week here,
so we're actually asking anyone

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.968
if they have
any grandma stories.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:20.601
And you have a very interesting
story of your grandma.

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:23.601
-Well, it's actually my mum. I
didn't know one of my grannies.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:26.067
The other one lived in South
Africa, but my mother started

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:29.701
the first ever exercise-based
school in England in the '70s.

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:31.133
-First ever exercise-based
school.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.901
-Yes, and it was called
Granny's Beautiful Bodies.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:35.534
-Yeah.
-That's my granny story.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.801
-Yeah, your mom was
Granny's Beautiful Bodies.

00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:40.868
-But she was not a granny.
She's a granny now.

00:00:40.868 --> 00:00:41.901
Gan-Gan.

00:00:41.901 --> 00:00:43.367
-Oh, that's the name?
-Gan-Gan.

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.100
-Everyone's got a name.
-We've got a Gaga and a Gan-Gan.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.734
-Oh, Gaga and a Gan-Gan.
-Gaga and Gan-Gan.

00:00:46.734 --> 00:00:48.934
-♪ Oh, gaga da gang gang gong
gida gangy gong gong ♪

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:51.734
♪ Gang gang gong gong gang
gang gong gong gang gang ♪

00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:54.334
What was the school about?
-I love this stuff.

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:55.901
-I know.
-What was the school?

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:57.501
So it was this very -- It was
kind of like

00:00:57.501 --> 00:01:01.334
a yoga/Alexander Technique
combo, ballet, balletic.

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:03.767
But like Albert Finney went
and Ava Gardner.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:05.133
It was super glam.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.767
-Wow.
-Yeah. She was really good.

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.133
-And did you ever take
any classes?

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.367
-Never. Not once.
-No, of course not.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:11.634
-But actually, you know what?

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:12.868
So she had the school and then

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:14.567
they had kids and they sort of
moved away.

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.834
And then when I was a teenager,
she set up --

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:18.701
she decided to start
teaching classes again.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:22.067
So she'd have these women -- I
was just like a moody teenager

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:24.434
in my room, and there would be
these women next door, like,

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:25.634
going --
[ Breathing heavily ]

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:27.100
You know, stretching and doing
the stuff.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.634
And then it was so popular that
they'd be out in the landing

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:32.467
and I was this horrible
14-year-old with a hoodie on

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:34.701
and I'd just climb over these
women with their legs in the air

00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:36.868
to go to the loo and, like,
slam the door.

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:38.567
-"Get out of my house!"
-They still came.

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:39.767
She was obviously that good.

00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:42.067
But no, I've never done
one class with her.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.801
-I wanted to ask you
because you just recently

00:01:43.801 --> 00:01:45.834
celebrated a milestone birthday.

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:47.300
-Yeah. 21.
-21.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.868
[ Laughter ]
Wow, congratulations.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:52.000
You can have alcohol if you want
to try, you can do it.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.767
What is it, 40?
-No, 21.

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:56.400
-That's right, yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.968
-What is wrong with you?
-I don't know.

00:01:57.968 --> 00:01:59.367
"What is wrong with you?
Why do you keep saying 40?"

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.100
-I turned 40.
What's the matter with you?

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.734
-What is wrong with me? Come on.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:04.634
-I am 40 years old.
-Did you celebrate?

00:02:04.634 --> 00:02:07.200
Did you have a party?
-I have the worst birthday.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.701
I'm born on
the 28th of December,

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:11.634
which I was driving
actually this year

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:14.300
and the radio man said, "And
here we are in the black hole

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.534
between Christmas and New Year,
the 28th of December."

00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:18.601
-[ Crying ]
Happy birthday to me!

00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:20.767
-No one's ever put it
like that before.

00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:23.901
But it's true. It's the whole
excitement, and then Boxing Day,

00:02:23.901 --> 00:02:25.634
and then the 27th,
you're starting to get low.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:27.901
By the 28th, you're like --
you're down.

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:30.934
You're down. And then I'm --
Yeah, I'm the loser that

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:33.267
then has to, like, open presents
again in front of people.

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:34.767
The whole thing is,
like, mortifying,

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:37.634
so I never have a party,
although I was peer-pressured

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:39.634
to organize one extensively

00:02:39.634 --> 00:02:42.834
that I had to cancel [Cackles]
because of omicron.

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:44.834
Not that that's
a good thing but...

00:02:44.834 --> 00:02:47.868
Yeah, but it was ripping through
England and I had organized

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:51.100
a 1920s birthday party
for the "Roaring Twenties"

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.767
that we have been enjoying.

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.534
-Yeah, right.
-Yeah.

00:02:54.534 --> 00:02:55.968
-But wait.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:58.567
There's something satisfying
about canceling plans.

00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:01.834
-Actually, my best friend and I
had an idea to set up a company

00:03:01.834 --> 00:03:05.834
where we extensively and
vigorously plan an event for you

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:08.767
that is incredibly stressful and
we cancel it the night before.

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.133
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:11.501
-So you can just relax
and you're like,

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:12.868
"Ah, let's just have
a glass of wine."

00:03:12.868 --> 00:03:14.033
-The feeling of like --

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:15.968
Anyone who cancels on me
is my favorite person.

00:03:15.968 --> 00:03:17.701
[ Laughter ]
And I guess it's my age.

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.167
-That's so funny.

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:20.801
-I don't want ever
to do anything.

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:23.868
-That is so funny.
Do you remember celebrating

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:25.868
your birthday
when we did a movie together?

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:27.367
"Factory Girl"?
-Yes.

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:28.734
-Yeah.
-You ate my face.

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:30.400
On a cake. On a cake.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:36.734
-Well, we'll be right back.

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:38.534
No, I had your --
-Yes.

00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:40.000
-Yes, I had your --
-My face was on a cake.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.267
-Yes, we put a giant --
-You did not eat my face.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:45.567
-But technically I guess we --
-You did go for the face.

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.667
-Yeah, I did.
I thought that'd be funny.

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:48.934
-Yeah, because
you're a funny guy.

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:50.033
-Well, I thought
that was very --

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:51.534
It was a nice, glamorous shot
of you.

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:54.400
-It was not the prettiest
picture, was it?

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.434
They had found a picture of me
that was a wardrobe test photo

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:00.367
that had been leaked from
"G.I. Joe."

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:01.567
Like, not even
an officially approved --

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:03.834
Someone had, like, leaked
a bad one.

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:06.000
I had, like, the wrong hair and,
like, black leather --

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.534
Holly Wiersma,
our great producer.

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:09.234
-Was it?
-I think she got the cake.

00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:10.501
-Yeah, and so she wanted to make
sure you had the best --

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:13.534
-So we ate an ugly picture
of me, yeah.

00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:15.767
The time of our lives.
That was my college years.

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:17.934
-It was, right?
-Shreveport, Louisiana.

00:04:17.934 --> 00:04:20.200
-We had the best time together,
yeah.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.968
But now look, we're parents.

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:23.400
-Now we're 21 and getting older.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.234
-You're 21 years old and wow.

00:04:25.234 --> 00:04:27.367
-The good old days.
-Yeah.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.434
Marlowe is so fun.

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:30.834
Here's what my question for you.

00:04:30.834 --> 00:04:32.167
What do you play for her?

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:33.601
She's 10 years old?

00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:35.501
-So, we used to do this thing
when I drove her to school.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:38.033
We now live in New York
and there's a school bus. Yes.

00:04:38.033 --> 00:04:40.434
But in England, I used to drive
her to school,

00:04:40.434 --> 00:04:42.601
and I decided to do, like,
an album a week

00:04:42.601 --> 00:04:45.534
to musically educate
my 3-year-old at the time.

00:04:45.534 --> 00:04:47.367
-Did it work?
-I think so.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:48.834
-Yeah, that's good.
-I mean, I have no say

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:50.801
in what she listens to now,
but back then we did like

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:53.033
pet sounds
and we did the Smiths.

00:04:53.033 --> 00:04:55.801
She used to sing like,
"Happiness is a Warm Gun"

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.100
at three, which is funny.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.701
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:02.100
We tried --
We try to sneak those in

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.901
and then they just kind of
went to their own area.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:06.667
-What do they --
What are they into?

00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:09.901
-They're into, like,
hard-core metal, heavy metal.

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:12.400
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.400
I want to talk about
"Anatomy of a Scandal."

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.133
-Or a sandal.
That's what we called it on set.

00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:17.934
-Academy -- Anatomy of a S--
-Academy of a Sandal.

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.200
-Academy of Sandals.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.467
And the sandal goes to --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:23.000
"Anatomy of a Scandal."

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.400
-Yes.
-David E. Kelley.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.701
-Yeah.
-Not too shabby.

00:05:25.701 --> 00:05:27.467
-Not too shabby.
The guy can write.

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:29.834
We'll give him that.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:31.701
-He co-wrote it
with Melissa James Gibson,

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:34.000
who is also a sublime writer.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.033
-And you told me that you --
Currently, you worked with

00:05:37.033 --> 00:05:39.100
one of the biggest stars you've
ever worked with in your career.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.334
-Yeah.
-Now, this is --

00:05:40.334 --> 00:05:41.834
I mean, you've worked with me.
-Yeah.

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:43.434
-And you're saying --
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:43.434 --> 00:05:44.701
-You're saying
this is a bigger moment.

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:46.000
-Bradley Cooper, Clint Eastwood.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.934
But whatever because I worked
with Peppa Pig.

00:05:48.934 --> 00:05:50.334
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.334 --> 00:05:52.467
Amelie.
-Amelie Bea Smith.

00:05:52.467 --> 00:05:54.167
This is the voice of Peppa Pig?

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:55.667
-That is the voice of Peppa Pig.

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:58.167
And I've never fangirled out
on anyone.

00:05:58.167 --> 00:06:01.334
Like, it was just --
It was really too much.

00:06:01.334 --> 00:06:04.334
I'd sit there across set with
my cup sort of staring at her.

00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:07.033
And then make her FaceTime
my daughter relentlessly,

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.200
and she was such a pro,
you know? She was like nine.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.834
-You're like,
"Just say 'muddy puddles!'

00:06:10.834 --> 00:06:13.567
Say 'muddy puddles!'"
-Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:16.334
I have all these voice notes.
Like, "Hello, Marlowe.

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:18.167
This Is --"
I was like, "Yes!"

00:06:18.167 --> 00:06:21.133
-That's why we work so hard,
to get to this spot.

00:06:21.133 --> 00:06:24.300
-Exactly.
-How do we set up the series?

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.367
-So, this series is --
It's the people that did

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:28.667
"Big Little Lies"
and "The Undoing,"

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:31.901
and it's that six-part format --
-I love both of them.

00:06:31.901 --> 00:06:33.501
I love both of them. Come on.

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:35.434
-They didn't even play for that
because it's such a crush.

00:06:35.434 --> 00:06:37.501
-Oh, it's such a crush.
-So, these people know

00:06:37.501 --> 00:06:40.167
how to do this, and this one is
about a sort of --

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:43.133
I'm the wife of
a Tory politician in England

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:45.367
who is caught having an affair

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:48.100
and it evolves into
all sorts of drama.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.834
It's Michelle Dockery, costars
Naomi Scott, Rupert Friend.

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:54.534
This scene I think is
when the bomb is dropped.

00:06:54.534 --> 00:06:58.033
-Yeah, and he has to tell you
before the scandal gets out,

00:06:58.033 --> 00:06:59.901
"Hey, this happened.
I had an affair."

00:06:59.901 --> 00:07:01.667
-Yeah.
-"So let's squash this.

00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:02.901
Let's figure this out
before it gets --

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:04.200
It's going to be out
in the press.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.267
It's going to be everywhere.
I just want you to know."

00:07:06.267 --> 00:07:09.501
-It was, like,
such a horrible moment.

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:12.067
I'm sure many people have
experienced that moment of,

00:07:12.067 --> 00:07:13.434
like, this thing has happened,

00:07:13.434 --> 00:07:16.634
and my heartbeat
started to go up,

00:07:16.634 --> 00:07:17.801
and I had a microphone on,

00:07:17.801 --> 00:07:20.067
and they could hear
my heartbeat accelerating

00:07:20.067 --> 00:07:22.434
on the cans when they were
watching the monitor.

00:07:22.434 --> 00:07:23.601
-Really?
-And they've actually --

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:25.000
So, they kept that in
and then they use it

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.133
as a motif thing
throughout the show.

00:07:27.133 --> 00:07:28.734
My little heartbeat.

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:31.133
-Your heartbeat is in the scene?
-Yeah, that's how I act.

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:33.534
-Come on.
That's how Sienna Miller --

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:35.300
You don't mess with
Sienna Miller, man.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.667
She'll tell you what's up.
Put the mike by the heart.

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:40.267
"I'll make the heart -- How loud
do you want the heartbeat?

00:07:40.267 --> 00:07:41.467
I can do it."

00:07:41.467 --> 00:07:42.701
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:07:42.701 --> 00:07:44.901
Here's Sienna Miller
in "Anatomy of a Scandal."

00:07:44.901 --> 00:07:46.234
Take a look.

00:07:46.234 --> 00:07:48.100
[ Heart beating ]

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.567
-There's more.

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:55.868
You're not telling me just to
tell me, to unburden yourself.

00:07:55.868 --> 00:07:57.400
There's more.

00:07:59.667 --> 00:08:00.934
Is she pregnant?

00:08:00.934 --> 00:08:03.501
-No. God, no.

00:08:03.501 --> 00:08:06.267
This story is about to break.

00:08:06.267 --> 00:08:08.367
-When?
-Tonight.

00:08:08.367 --> 00:08:10.601
The Mail is splashing it
tomorrow, online late tonight.

00:08:10.601 --> 00:08:12.167
Chris Clark's on his way over.

00:08:12.167 --> 00:08:14.467
-He's coming here? Now?

00:08:14.467 --> 00:08:15.934
-We have to get ahead of this.

00:08:15.934 --> 00:08:18.467
-We have to figure out how to
protect our children from it.

00:08:18.467 --> 00:08:20.667
-We have to do both.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.067
-[ Imitating heartbeat ]

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:24.901
-Sienna Miller!

00:08:24.901 --> 00:08:28.801
"Anatomy of a Scandal" begins
streaming April 15th on Netflix.

